Goals and future plans | “Will” and “be going to” can express future plans or predictions with different contexts. Name a realistic action and time so readers know what is planned. | “I am going to read one chapter tonight” states a plan; “I will help you” can be a decision made while speaking. | Write two sentences about a study plan for next week. | Example: “I am going to review science on Monday. I will ask for help if I need it.” | Two classmates plan a group project with different available times. | Write a short exchange proposing a time and checking the other person's availability. | The exchange names a day and time, allows a reply, and changes the plan if needed.
Describing processes | Process texts explain how something works in a logical sequence. Passive forms can focus on the process when the actor is less important than the action. | “Paper is folded, then it is cut” focuses on steps; safety notes are needed if cutting tools are involved. | Rewrite “Students sort the cards” to focus on the cards. | “The cards are sorted by students” is passive; the actor may be omitted if not needed. | A class explains how borrowed books are returned and checked without naming every staff member. | Write four process steps with sequence words and a final check. | Steps should cover returning, checking condition, recording return, and placing books for the next reader.
Narrative and character choice | A narrative links events through characters' choices and consequences. A summary keeps the key problem and change rather than listing every detail. | A character apologizes after losing a shared book; the apology matters because it changes how the friends solve the problem. | Identify the problem and decision in that example. | The book is lost; the character decides to apologize and help solve the loss. | Read a short original story about a missed meeting and a repaired friendship. | Write a three-sentence summary with beginning, choice, and outcome. | The summary preserves cause and effect without adding events that did not appear in the story.
Information reports | A report describes a general topic using organized facts rather than a single personal experience. Check definitions, examples, and sources for claims. | A report on mangroves may explain their habitat and role; one visit to one beach does not prove conditions at every coast. | Explain one difference between a general report and a personal recount. | A report organizes general facts with sources; a recount tells events experienced in a time order. | Your class prepares an English fact sheet about a local public place using approved sources. | Write a short heading, two verified facts, and a source line. | Facts must match the source and avoid private or unverified claims.
Suggestions and obligations | “Should” gives advice, while “must” signals a stronger obligation or rule. Use wording appropriate to the situation and do not invent rules. | “You should check the source” is advice; “You must wear a helmet” may state a safety rule when cycling under applicable rules. | Write one suggestion and one rule for keeping a shared study room clean. | Example: “You should put papers away.” “You must follow the room's posted safety rules.” | A friend plans to post photos from a group activity online without asking others. | Write a polite suggestion and explain the privacy reason. | “You should ask everyone first” respects consent; do not publish a person's image without appropriate permission.
Presenting an idea | A short presentation needs a clear point, supporting details, and a closing invitation for questions. Speak at a pace listeners can follow and label any images used. | “Our club plans a book exchange. It needs a table and labeled boxes. We welcome questions” has a clear beginning and end. | Prepare three sentences presenting one school activity to new students. | The sentences should state the activity, one useful detail, and how to join or ask questions. | The group presents a plan for a reading corner in English. Members divide speaking roles. | Write an outline with introduction, two reasons, and closing. | Each speaker has a clear role; the closing summarizes the proposal and invites clarification.
